# Part 1 - the hard rules

These are inviolable. A change that violates one is not a tradeoff to discuss;
it is work to throw away. Keep the list short enough to memorize.

**1. <RULE_TITLE>.** <One paragraph. State the rule, then the single sentence
that says why it exists. Example shape: "Everything runs locally by default.
A remote call is a deliberate, named, reversible exception - because the thing
we are protecting is <WHAT>.">

**2. <RULE_TITLE>.** <...>

**3. Honest state.** A component reports ready, ok, or available only at its
genuine capability. A stub says it is a stub, in the data and on the screen. A
failed or malformed read is never rendered as a healthy zero. Silence is not
success.

**4. Provenance on every claim.** <Anything the system asserts carries where it
came from and how confident it is. Adjust to your domain or delete.>

**5. Reversibility.** Every automated change ships with the way to undo it: a
flag that disables it, a documented rollback, or both. A change that cannot be
reversed needs the owner's explicit say-so before it lands.

**6. <RULE_TITLE>.** <...>

*(Add or remove rules freely. Six to twelve is the range that stays read.)*

