# Part 2 - operating rules

These govern how work moves. They came from measured failures; each cites one.

## 2.1 One pull request in the pipeline at a time

Work is built in parallel; it is **integrated in series**. One designated
branch, one open pull request, one set of checks running.

- Do not push to a branch whose checks are still running. A push cancels the
  in-flight run and restarts the meter.
- Do not open a second pull request while one is in review or in CI.
- Units built in parallel are **transplanted** onto the moved base one at a
  time, in the merge order the coordinator sets.

*Why:* concurrent heads cancel each other's runs and burn the review lane's
quota with nothing to show. The reference deployment measured eleven cancelled
runs across seventy-seven minutes on a single pull request before adopting this.

## 2.2 Identity is per-invocation, never shared configuration

**Agents never write `git config`.** Repository-level git configuration is
shared mutable state: one agent's write lands on another agent's in-flight
commit.

Every commit sets identity on the invocation instead:

```
git -c user.name="<AGENT_DISPLAY_NAME>" -c user.email="<AGENT_EMAIL>" commit ...
```

or the equivalent `GIT_AUTHOR_NAME` / `GIT_AUTHOR_EMAIL` /
`GIT_COMMITTER_NAME` / `GIT_COMMITTER_EMAIL` environment variables.

The same rule covers every other shared mutable surface in a shared tree:
**temp files are keyed by agent key** (`commitmsg-<agent_key>.txt`, not
`commitmsg.txt`), and no agent reads a temp file it did not write.

*Why:* observed directly - a configuration write changed a sibling's commit
authorship, and a sibling overwrote a shared commit-message file between write
and use, so a commit briefly carried the wrong unit's message.

## 2.3 Merge authority is the coordinator's, and stays human-side

Only the top-level session flips a pull request out of draft and merges it.
Builders report green and stop.

A check that reads green is not necessarily the check that gates the merge.
Before merging, confirm the **name** of the green check is the one branch
protection requires - a reduced or draft-scoped run can be green and still not
be the full gate.

*Why:* this caught two would-have-been-early merges in the reference
deployment's first wave, both on draft-scoped checks that read green.

## 2.4 Gates run last

Static analysis, generated-file checks, budget checks and the test suite run
**after** your final edit, not before it. A gate run before the last change
proves nothing about what you are shipping.

*Why:* a unit shipped a stale generated file exactly this way.

## 2.5 Evidence over self-report

Status comes from the repository, the tests and the checks - not from an agent
describing itself. A run that claims it is working, past its deadline, with no
terminal record, is **stale**, whatever it said about itself.

Before accepting any agent's report of a check result, read the check.

## 2.6 Scope is declared, and it is a ceiling

Each unit of work declares its file surface before it starts and touches
nothing outside it. Something worth fixing outside your surface is **filed**,
not fixed. An out-of-scope fix is how two units collide in one tree.

## 2.7 Questions escalate; they do not get guessed

When a change requires a decision the brief does not contain, stop and raise a
question against the item. Continue only on work that does not depend on the
answer. Record the answer in the decision log - one file per ruling - so it is
not re-litigated.

## 2.8 Review threads have exactly one owner

The unit that owns the pull request owns its review threads. The coordinator
takes a thread only by telling that unit first.

Verify a finding before accepting **or** declining it, and prove the
disagreement when you decline - a reproduction, a counterfactual test, a
citation. A suggested fix may be declined in favour of a better one that is
shown to be better.

*Why:* two agents sharing one platform identity answered the same review thread
minutes apart, producing a contradictory double reply.

## 2.9 Metered lanes are named, not waited on

A review lane that is rate-limited, quota-exhausted or slower than
<WAIT_BUDGET_MINUTES> minutes is **named in the report and proceeded past**.
Never push an empty commit to re-trigger one; that spends the meter and pushes
the window further out.

The price of skipping a reviewer is that its job is now yours, in full: edge
cases tested, every found defect regression-tested, every test mutation-checked
(revert the fix, confirm the test fails, restore), and everything run locally
before the push.

## 2.10 Subagents do not schedule, and do not persist

A subagent ends its run and reports. It does not register timers or check-ins:
those fire into the parent session, so the agent that scheduled one waits
forever while its wake-up wakes somebody else.

Only the coordinator schedules. Persistence lives on disk, not in an agent.

## 2.11 Definition of done

A unit is done when all of these are true:

- [ ] The acceptance in the brief is met, and demonstrated - not asserted.
- [ ] Tests cover the new behaviour **and** its failure branch; each new test
      is mutation-checked.
- [ ] Every found defect has a regression test landing with its fix.
- [ ] Nothing outside the declared file surface changed.
- [ ] Generated or derived files are regenerated by their tool, never hand-edited.
- [ ] If a budget-counted context document grew, its budget check was run and
      the measured number recorded (see `BUDGETED_DOCS.md`).
- [ ] Gates ran last and are green.
- [ ] The wall reflects reality: the item's state, the pull request number, the
      events.
- [ ] <PROJECT_SPECIFIC_DOD_ITEM>.

## 2.12 A standing directive is recorded in the same working chunk

When the owner issues a rule, a threshold or a correction mid-session, it is
written into the surface that binds - this file for a rule, the configuration
for a threshold, a decision record for a ruling - **in the same working chunk it
was given**, not at session close and not "next time we touch that file".

A directive that exists only in a conversation is a bug with a known outcome:
the session that heard it ends, and the next one re-learns the rule by breaking
it. The handoff report is not the binding surface either; it is a handoff, and
the next session is required to vet it rather than obey it.

The same rule covers a *changed* requirement. It updates every surface that
carries the old one, and where the new requirement contradicts a recorded one,
the two are **quoted side by side and escalated** - never silently layered,
never deleted, and never read in whichever direction suits the work in flight.
Superseding marks are written in **both** directions, so a reader landing on
either surface can tell which is current without reading both.

*Why:* a rule given out loud and obeyed for an afternoon is indistinguishable,
the following day, from a rule that was never given.

